WHY DO WE NEED MULTIPLE LLM EVALUATION PIPELINES?
-------------------------------------------------

- An LLM-powered AI application or agent can have multiple failure points.

- One eval pipeline can focus on the retriever and another on the generator.

- Suppose we've confirmed that the retriever and the generator each work fine
  on their own. Does that mean the RAG app as a whole will work fine?
  Not necessarily.

- For example, the retriever might correctly fetch 5 chunks from the vector DB
  (since k = 5). These 5 chunks plus the query are sent to the generator (LLM).
  Because of the system prompt, the generator focuses on d1 and d2, but the
  actual answer was in d5, so it gives the wrong answer.

- So we need another evaluation on the combination of retriever and generator.

- We might also need re-ranking. Individual components can work fine while the
  combination still fails.

- Failures can occur at the component level, the workflow level, or the entire
  application level, so we need evals at all three levels.


LLM APPLICATION EVAL - RISK CATEGORIES
--------------------------------------

1. Application Quality - "Is the answer any good?"
   Whether the app does its actual job well: gives correct, relevant, complete
   answers that do what the user asked. This is the core "does it work"
   question, the thing the product exists to do.

2. Safety - "Could the answer cause harm?"
   Whether the app avoids hurting anyone or exposing anything it shouldn't:
   no toxic or dangerous content, no bias against groups, no leaking of private
   data, and no getting tricked into breaking its own rules. This isn't about
   whether the answer is good, but whether it's harmful.

3. Operational - "Is it fast, cheap, and reliable enough to run?"
   Whether the app is practical to operate at scale: it responds quickly,
   doesn't cost too much per answer, and doesn't crash or fail under real
   traffic. This isn't about what the answer says, but about the system
   delivering it.

The clean way to tell them apart:
   Quality     -> the CONTENT of the answer
   Safety      -> the HARM the answer could do
   Operational -> the SYSTEM producing it


=================================================================
1. APPLICATION QUALITY
=================================================================

1a. Core Generation Quality (evaluates the LLM)
-----------------------------------------------
Correctness / accuracy     Whether the factual claims in the answer are
                           actually true.
Relevance                  Whether the answer addresses what the user
                           actually asked.
Completeness               Whether the answer covers all parts of a
                           multi-part question.
Instruction following      Whether the output obeys explicit constraints
                           (format, length, etc.).


1b. RAG-Specific Quality
------------------------
Context relevance          Whether the retrieved chunks are actually
                           relevant to the query.
Retrieval recall           Whether all chunks needed to answer were
                           retrieved.
Groundedness /             Whether every claim is supported by the
faithfulness               retrieved context.
Citation accuracy          Whether cited sources genuinely support the
                           claims made.


1c. Agent / Tool-Use Quality
----------------------------
Tool selection             Whether the agent chose the right tool for the
                           task.
Parameter correctness      Whether the tool was called with valid, correctly
                           typed arguments.
Task completion            Whether the agent actually accomplished the
                           user's goal.
Error recovery             Whether it handled tool failures gracefully and
                           retried sensibly.


1d. Multi-Turn Conversation Quality
-----------------------------------
Context retention /        Whether it remembers relevant facts given earlier
memory                     in the conversation.
Clarification behavior     Whether it asks for clarification when the
                           request is ambiguous.


=================================================================
2. SAFETY
=================================================================
Toxicity                   Whether the output contains offensive, abusive,
                           or hateful language.
Harmful content            Whether it provides dangerous instructions
                           (self-harm, weapons, illegal acts).
Bias / fairness            Whether outputs are free of unfair treatment or
                           stereotyping across groups.
PII leakage                Whether it exposes personal or private data.
Prompt injection /         Whether it resists attempts to override its
jailbreak resistance       rules or bypass guardrails.


=================================================================
3. OPERATIONAL
=================================================================
End-to-end latency         Total time from user request to final response.
Cost per request           Total token/compute spend to produce one
                           response.
Token efficiency           Whether it achieves its result without wasteful
                           token usage.
Error / failure rate       Fraction of requests that fail, time out, or
                           return malformed output.
Latency under load         Whether latency stays acceptable at production
                           concurrency.
